Z||000000||Mr.Frosty||04-11-2001||03:01 PM||||First,Let me first say that I know this subject has been beaten to death,and I've done extensive searching with the search engine,and can't find what I need.I need to talk to someone with a similar setup.What kind of numbers does your turbo produce on the dyno?How long have you had the setup?Did you rebuild the engine before or after?If so did you use forged goodies inside?Is the compression ratio changed at all?I am going to get mine rebuilt soon and was wondering what kind of longevity do you get out of your motor?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000001||Predator||04-11-2001||04:21 PM||||I have the Design One Systems Stage II turbo kit. I had it Dyno at 165 hp 207 ftlbs torque at the wheels. It was running lean so the numbers may be a little better. The engine is stock. The kit is setup to run on a stock 2.8 liter. I would not have the compression raised. The stock fuel system is supposed to handle the system, but again I am running lean so I cannot say for sure. You can piece together a turbo system, and others around here have. I liked the kit. To each his/her own on that opinion. The car runs very strong. If you want more bang for the buck then you can go with a bigger turbo. The kit runs 9 to 10 lbs of boost which I was told is about all you want to pour into a stock motor. The turbo I have is small, but spools fast. It produces plenty of power for me. The engine should last as long with or without the turbo. It depends on how much you abuse it. If you just kick it every now and then it should be no problem. If you live with your foot to the floor then expect it to last as long as you would think that would last. In other words the turbo does not shorten the life of the engine the driver does.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||04-11-2001||05:06 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> what donald said..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I've got turbos.. but don't have the time to install anything... on a turbo car the compression for a daily drive should be around 8.5-9:1. and you'll have the ability to put SERIOUS boost to it. Donalds kits is KILLER! but he is having engine management problems. with either an additional injector, a Vortech SFMU, an MSD 6BTM you can build a HELLA crazy fun car if you have the time to mess with it for under the $4000+ for a kit.  what kinda HP/TQ numbers are you making and do you have the air/fuel curve plotted with your dyno graph?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mr.Frosty||04-11-2001||05:30 PM||||My car is a complete "was running a week ago" car.I have a Mitsu-water cooled t-3,and it's running about 8 or so pounds of boost.It has water meth injection and an extra fuel injector in the intake.It is hellaciously fast,but it runs lean also,and I think that's what killed the motor.Eric at West coast fiero has a different computer,Map sensor,etc, that is supposed to take care of that problem.I have a boost gauge pod on the side of the instrument panel,and it also has an oil cooler behind the passenger side air scoop.It kicks ass in street races,but it needs to be properly tuned.I thought the stock compression ratio was 8.5:1,so leaving it stock would be right then huh?I'm going to beef up the internals when I rebuild,so I don't have to do this again anytime soon.Do you have yours intercooled? If so where did you put the intercooler?<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000004||Predator||04-11-2001||06:36 PM||||Sounds like a nice setup. I don't have it Intercooled yet, but I am working on figuring out where to put the intercooler, and arranging the plumbing for it. I will probably take out the air can, and put it there with a fan under it. I have looked at the lazy C scoops, and I am considering using one of them there as well. I have a fan over the battery that blows air out of the compartment so I hope that with the air coming in one side and out the other it will provide good cooling. I am going to try and talk Travis into helping me with the intercooler at some point. The problem now is getting the lean condition fixed. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000005||86GT Turbo||04-12-2001||01:30 AM||||Another thing you might what to consider is detonation <BR>I installed a miller woods tubo on my GT<BR>when it had 60K on it and it did not last<BR>more then 5K even running 94 octane<BR>I burned up 2 pistons and a head<BR>know I run a forged pistons a custom ground <BR>cam,SI valves,and ported heads with <BR>water/alcohol injection. at 11 psig boost<BR>I had my car put on a dyno and  now I'm getting 246hp at 220 ft/lb of torque<BR>on a standard day<BR>the water injection is what I found to work the best to get rid of detonation.<P>just my 2 cents<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||04-12-2001||08:03 AM||||a Haltech E6GM or E6K would allow you to manipulate your fuel curves to cover the boost. if your looking at a Split second <A HREF="http://www.splitsec.com" TARGET=_blank>www.splitsec.com</A>   fuel tuning module.. don't waste your $. have you run your car on a dyno to see how lean it really is/was? ||||reg||1
