Z||000011||1FST2M6||04-13-2001||08:08 AM||||the guys at PCA (atl) Porsche Club of America (Atlanta chapter) have - HAD - 3 installed in both turbo and non turbo cars and the units will allow you to tune the low end and the high end but the mid range usually causes a really bad spike deep into the lean spectrum (14-15:1). this unit (ARC2) was also used by grassroots motorsports on thier project E30 M3. the unit was installed at the shop and caused more fuel problems than they had before. the split second stuff is nice and i'm sure it works in some cars but none that i've seen. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mr.Frosty||04-13-2001||04:13 PM||||I forgot to ask,does anybody use a blowoff/bypass valve on their turbos?If so what brand and type?Are these just gimmickery,or are they only necessary for high boost apps.? Predator,your water/alchahol injection system what kind of pump are you using? I'm using a windshield washer pump.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top-ForSale <BR>85 GT Turbo-project car||||reg||10
Z||000013||Phantom Rage||04-13-2001||05:25 PM||||Here’s the general Idea:<P>Blow-off valves are not gimmick’s.  They are used to relieve pressure when the throttle body butterfly is closed, usually in-between shifts.  What happens is the turbo is still spooled up in-between shifts and since the butterfly is closed the boost pressure has no where to go except back into the turbo, which is bad for the turbo’s longevity as well as response. <P>You can use it for any turbo set up; it does not have to be used just for high boost applications.<P>If you are just building a mock up turbo system and running low boost for now, I recommend picking one up at the junkyard.  Talon/Eclipse blow-off valves are pretty hefty and can handle a decent amount of boost, but are harder to find.  What is easier to find, but can’t take as much boost are the Bosch units found in Chrysler/Dodge turbo two, 2.2L motors.  You can find them inside the air box filter assembly. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000014||Mr.Frosty||04-13-2001||05:50 PM||||I understand the general idea behind them,as I tend to read those rice tuner turbo magazines from time to time..oops..But I have yet to hear anyone in this forum even mention them.I do a search and it turns up info on "valves" and "blow off you hoser,eh"<BR>Just a side note,but the Dodge/Chrysler blow offs,how can they be in the airbox if they're supposed to be in "front" of the turbo to prevent pressure spikes.Does that mean the turbo pushes air through the air filter before it goes into the engine? Just a thought.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top-ForSale <BR>85 GT Turbo-project car||||reg||10
Z||000015||Mr.Frosty||04-13-2001||05:53 PM||||Also I said gimmeckry,because a lot of those supposed tuning items in the ricer community I feel are mainly "fluff" items and are cosmetic.Just my .02  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top-ForSale <BR>85 GT Turbo-project car||||reg||10
Z||000016||Phantom Rage||04-13-2001||07:17 PM||||Yes, they are in the air box (turbo 2’s).  I can’t remember the exact configuration, but I know it was connected to rubber hoses.  One to read vacuum and the other to vent pressure to the bov.  The side that releases the pressure vents back into the intake from within the air box.  This may have been done to keep it quiet, perhaps even something to do with emissions.<P>Think about this.  Major automobile manufactures such as Chrysler/Dodge, Nissan, Subaru, and Mitsubishi use them and they don’t vent into atmospheric for that cool ppsssssssshhhhhhh sound.  Maybe there is something to be said about bov’s.    <P>I spent over $100 for my Turbonetic’s bov, and then I found the Chrysler/Dodge ones at the junkyard.  I got it for just $5 as a spare.  So if you really want to know for your self if they work or not, just spend a little time and a few bucks at your local junkyard. <P>||||reg||7
Z||000017||Turbo1dr||04-14-2001||12:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>LOL!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.haltech.com" TARGET=_blank>www.haltech.com</A>  you'll need a 3bar map sensor the or the stock unit from a sunbird turbo or grand national. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  to find a Dyno center near you... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Actually, the Grand National uses a MAF sensor instead of a MAP sensor. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Sillhouette.com||04-14-2001||12:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FST2M6:<BR><B>LOL!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.haltech.com" TARGET=_blank>www.haltech.com</A>  you'll need a 3bar map sensor the or the stock unit from a sunbird turbo or grand national. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  to find a Dyno center near you... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wow a 3 BAR map sensor that's a bit much only usable if you run the turbo at 30psi and I know none of your engine parts will hold up for that no matter how much money you spend on strengthening it.  There is only one engine that I've seen run 34psi and that is the GNX.  Obviously they had to run a MAF sensor since 34psi is above the 3BAR capabilities.  The sunbird ones are 2BAR good to 15psi.  <P>Later<P>||||reg||1
